2.m:
Addressed thus, he divided himself into two, female and male;
and he split the male form tenfold and as one.
Thence the eleven Rudras, born from Brahmā, became renowned.

6.m:
Instantly the eminent brāhmaṇa died, and you, O king, were filled with joy,
thinking, 'This deer has been slain,' as long as you looked, O ruler of the earth;
by that time the brāhmaṇa with the deer's body was dead upon the mountain of the waterfall.

8.m:
[Grains] sown increase a thousandfold every year, O sage.
I kill a single animal in the forest every day for the sake of my family;
if I consecrate that, offer it to the ancestors, and eat it along with my followers—

9.m:
Then, when the night had passed, the lotus-eyed one arose;
he thought of the Vedas and of the mother in all four [quarters],
[yet] as he pondered, the lord of gods did not perceive the Vedas.

10.m:
He worshiped with devotion his own passionate form,
which has the nature of Time, terrible by nature, holding the trident in hand;
and through the quality of darkness he established himself among the asuras.

Having thus considered, the wise king, assembling an army
accompanied by elephants, horses, chariots, and foot-soldiers, marched to the northern region;
and all the regions to the north were subdued by that great-souled king.

Filled with astonishment on seeing them, wondering “Who are these fair-eyed ones?”,
while he remained thus for a single moment in that forest,
he caught sight of two ascetics.

12.m:
You ever, O God, create the splendours of all luminous bodies, bearer of all forms;
you have five qualities in the earth, four kinds in water, three kinds in fire,
twofold in air, and established in space;
you, O Hari, are the Spirit whose body is sound.
